.\evidence\ai\registers\ben-swartz.csv:19:"2026-09-10","Ben Swartz","Claude Code; Claude Web","Claude Opus 5","Regression investigation; measurement; debugging; code generation; test generation; documentation","Issue #430: identify why published match statistics intermittently fail to load on fixture detail pages and correct the underlying cause","A restored connection bound on the shared PostgreSQL pool; non-fatal cache-aside read and write in the fixture-statistics service so a disposable cache cannot fail an authoritative response; an error boundary and a reported failure reason in the public statistics section; pool, service, database and frontend regression tests; docs/statistics/fixture-statistics.md; evidence/validation/issue-430-published-statistics-loading.md","Tested the issue's two stated hypotheses and disproved both. The fixture-statistics cache does not return empty on a miss, keeps no cross-request state, invalidates by version inside the writing transaction, and returns the known-good figures cold and warm from the deployed environment; a cold miss falls through to derivation. The frontend has no request timeout at all, confirmed in source and in the deployed JavaScript bundle, so a cold response cannot exceed one; the deployed cold first call was measured at 25.00 s and succeeded, and the #410 figures were not carried across because they were local and for a different endpoint. Characterised the deployed endpoint over 23 requests: cold 25.00 s, first call after idle 1.51 s and 2.43 s, warm cache miss 1.01-1.48 s, warm cache hit 0.265-0.435 s, every request HTTP 200; nine page-shaped concurrent bursts all returned 200; the deployed page rendered Singapore 139 from 124 accepted events and Thailand 96 from 122 in a real Chromium instance with no console or page errors. The reported failure did not reproduce against the deployed environment and no server log was available, which is stated rather than glossed. Established instead that commit 5d14c4f dropped connectionTimeoutMillis from the pool while issue #369's own evidence document records it as part of that fix, demonstrated that the shipped configuration leaves a stalled connection pending past 20,007 ms where the documented one rejects at 10,021 ms, and showed the existing pool test stayed green throughout because it asserted only min, max and idleTimeoutMillis, max passing on the driver default. Demonstrated that the application mounts no error boundary, so a contract-valid response that cannot be displayed reduces the whole page to an empty root element with no error state and no retry, which is the reported blank section. Every new test was confirmed to fail against the unchanged code. Backend unit and API tests passed 153/153; the database suite passed 145 with 2 skipped and 1 pre-existing unrelated dataset-release failure that fails identically before the change; the frontend suite passed 127/127 across three consecutive runs; type-check, lint and Prettier passed; knip and syncpack passed. Docker was unavailable so the database suite ran through the repository embedded-postgres runner, and hygiene:architecture could not run because dependency-cruiser does not support this Node 25 runtime. The changed code is not deployed, so no deployed verification of the fix is claimed.","Issue #430; evidence/validation/issue-430-published-statistics-loading.md; docs/statistics/fixture-statistics.md; evidence/validation/issue-369-idle-backend-latency.md; evidence/validation/issue-293-cache-performance.md; commit 5d14c4f; Pull Request pending"
