.\evidence\ai\registers\ben-swartz.csv:19:"2026-09-10","Ben Swartz","Claude Code; Claude Web","Claude Opus 5","Regression investigation; measurement; debugging; code generation; test generation; documentation","Issue #430: identify why published match statistics intermittently fail to load on fixture detail pages and correct the underlying cause","A restored connection bound on the shared PostgreSQL pool; non-fatal cache-aside read and write in the fixture-statistics service so a disposable cache cannot fail an authoritative response; an error boundary and a reported failure reason in the public statistics section; pool, service, database and frontend regression tests; docs/statistics/fixture-statistics.md; evidence/validation/issue-430-published-statistics-loading.md","Tested the issue's two stated hypotheses and disproved both. The fixture-statistics cache does not return empty on a miss, keeps no cross-request state, invalidates by version inside the writing transaction, and returns the known-good figures cold and warm from the deployed environment; a cold miss falls through to derivation. The frontend has no request timeout at all, confirmed in source and in the deployed JavaScript bundle, so a cold response cannot exceed one; the deployed cold first call was measured at 25.00 s and succeeded, and the #410 figures were not carried across because they were local and for a different endpoint. Characterised the deployed endpoint over 23 requests: cold 25.00 s, first call after idle 1.51 s and 2.43 s, warm cache miss 1.01-1.48 s, warm cache hit 0.265-0.435 s, every request HTTP 200; nine page-shaped concurrent bursts all returned 200; the deployed page rendered Singapore 139 from 124 accepted events and Thailand 96 from 122 in a real Chromium instance with no console or page errors. The reported failure did not reproduce against the deployed environment and no server log was available, which is stated rather than glossed. Established instead that commit 5d14c4f dropped connectionTimeoutMillis from the pool while issue #369's own evidence document records it as part of that fix, demonstrated that the shipped configuration leaves a stalled connection pending past 20,007 ms where the documented one rejects at 10,021 ms, and showed the existing pool test stayed green throughout because it asserted only min, max and idleTimeoutMillis, max passing on the driver default. Demonstrated that the application mounts no error boundary, so a contract-valid response that cannot be displayed reduces the whole page to an empty root element with no error state and no retry, which is the reported blank section. Every new test was confirmed to fail against the unchanged code. Backend unit and API tests passed 153/153; the database suite passed 145 with 2 skipped and 1 pre-existing unrelated dataset-release failure that fails identically before the change; the frontend suite passed 127/127 across three consecutive runs; type-check, lint and Prettier passed; knip and syncpack passed. Docker was unavailable so the database suite ran through the repository embedded-postgres runner, and hygiene:architecture could not run because dependency-cruiser does not support this Node 25 runtime. The changed code is not deployed, so no deployed verification of the fix is claimed.","Issue #430; evidence/validation/issue-430-published-statistics-loading.md; docs/statistics/fixture-statistics.md; evidence/validation/issue-369-idle-backend-latency.md; evidence/validation/issue-293-cache-performance.md; commit 5d14c4f; Pull Request pending"
.\evidence\ai\registers\ben-swartz.csv:20:"2026-09-11","Ben Swartz","Claude Code; Claude Web","Claude Opus 5","Code generation; test generation; documentation; review","Issue #476: show participant career totals on the player page using the existing participant aggregate endpoint","A career totals section on the player page reading the career level of GET /api/v1/participants/{participantId}/statistics; a public read client method for that endpoint; the #430 statistics error boundary generalised into a shared section boundary now covering both player page sections; six new frontend tests and corrections to three existing player page tests; an extended Playwright player journey; documentation in apps/frontend/src/pages/README.md, apps/frontend/src/features/README.md and docs/statistics/participant-aggregates.md","Read the aggregate module, contract and documentation before writing anything and reported three findings before implementing. Established from the controller, the resource response schema, the scope-only query schema and the single grouped statement that the endpoint returns one resource and does not page, so there is no cursor to follow and the multi-page coverage case does not apply, and asserted instead that the client sends neither cursor nor limit to it, since #467 and #469 were both cursor defects. Established that batting average and highest score are not derived and are not named in section 7 of the domain definition, a batting average needing dismissals as a batter where the statement counts only wickets credited as bowler, and a highest score needing a per-innings maximum where the statement never groups by innings; did not compute either in presentation code, where a maximum over the ten-record history page would repeat the #469 defect, and recorded that part of the issue as unsatisfied in the aggregate documentation. Established that the player page mounted no error boundary and demonstrated that a contract-valid match record whose competition identifier cannot be encoded into a link reduced the whole page to an empty root element; generalised the #430 boundary rather than adding a second one and wrapped both sections, a retry after a history display failure remounting the collection so it requests again. Found that the existing player page tests answered every unmatched request with an empty collection and the Playwright journey answered it with 404, so both would have stayed green while the new section showed only an error, and gave each a real aggregate response; one existing test would also have thrown on a second alert. Chose career figures that are neither equal to nor sums of the match history figures, so a page deriving them from the history would fail, and asserted each value against its own label. Asserted concurrency by holding both requests unanswered and requiring both to have been issued. Confirmed every new and extended test fails against the unchanged source, 7 of 32 failing and 25 passing, restoring the implementation afterwards from a hashed backup. Found the first frontend baseline failure in the dataset release tests to be a stale contracts build that passed once rebuilt, rather than a defect. The working checkout was switched to another branch outside this session partway through; confirmed that only evidence files differed between the two bases and returned to the issue branch before recording this row. npm run check exited 0 with backend unit 197/197, frontend 143/143, API 156/156, worker 22/22, contracts 136/136, deployment 19/19 and CI routing 41/41, Prettier, lint, type-check, OpenAPI lint and build passing; the database suite passed 147 with 2 skipped and none failing through the embedded-postgres runner, counts read from the output rather than the exit code, the aggregate derivation still issuing two statements; the Playwright player journey passed on desktop and mobile including its accessibility scan; knip and syncpack passed, and hygiene:architecture could not run because dependency-cruiser does not support this Node 25 runtime. The existing wait for the participant record before either section mounts was left unchanged as outside the issue. The change is not deployed, so no deployed verification is claimed.","Issue #476; Issues #285, #430, #467 and #469; docs/statistics/participant-aggregates.md; docs/requirements/sport-domain-definition.md; apps/frontend/src/pages/README.md; tests/e2e/player-overview.spec.ts; Pull Request pending"
